tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{SFT vs.\ regex: semantic understanding.}
A regex baseline (B5) achieves 99.33\% by exploiting a format marker, but a \emph{nomarker} ablation (\S\ref{sec:nomarker}) resolves this concern: removing markers drops accuracy by only ${\sim}$2pp (Qwen-7B 97.65\%, Qwen-1.5B 97.82\%), leaving a 4.04pp gap over the best non-trivial regex (93.61\%).
On 8 boundary samples where pass-labeled code triggers exceptions, SFT classifies 6/8 correctly vs.\ 0/8 for regex (McNemar $p{=}0.041$), confirming ${\sim}$91\% of the signal is semantic.

\paragraph{Tier hierarchy: practical implications.}
The two-tier structure (Tier~1 ${\geq}$99\%; Tier~2 = 86.1\%) suggests a fallback design: extract exception events first (median 150 tokens), resort to Tier~2 only when no exception is raised (+10.50pp over no-trace).
ML-based verdict prediction adds value in settings where the full suite cannot run: patch correctness prediction, flaky test detection, and offline trace analysis.

tool_args.old_string:
\paragraph{Calibration and Defect Prediction.}
Neural network calibration \citep{guo2017calibration}, extended to LLMs \citep{kadavath2022language,geng2024calibration,spiess2025calibration}, studies confidence-correctness alignment; our ECE ${<}$ 0.007 suggests compact inputs improve calibration.
Just-in-time defect prediction progressed from feature engineering \citep{kamei2013large} through deep learning \citep{hoang2019deepjit} to simpler-but-effective approaches \citep{pornprasit2021jitline}; these methods share our fault detection motivation but operate on code change features rather than execution traces.

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Calibration and Defect Prediction.}
Neural network calibration \citep{guo2017calibration}, extended to LLMs \citep{kadavath2022language,geng2024calibration,spiess2025calibration}, studies confidence-correctness alignment; our ECE ${<}$ 0.007 suggests compact inputs improve calibration.
Just-in-time defect prediction \citep{kamei2013large,hoang2019deepjit,pornprasit2021jitline} shares our fault detection motivation but operates on code change features rather than traces.

\paragraph{Fault Localization.}
Spectrum-based fault localization (SBFL) ranks program elements by suspiciousness from test spectra \citep{abreu2007accuracy,jones2002visualization,wong2014dstar}; recent LLM-based approaches \citep{kang2024autofl,qin2024agentfl,widyasari2024llm4fl,mukherjee2025llmfl} share our insight that anomalous execution events carry the strongest diagnostic signal.
Verdict prediction is a prerequisite to FL (determining \emph{whether} a fault exists before localizing \emph{where}), and both exploit the same signal type at different granularities.

